; When each culture's pronouns first entered the record — written, and spoken.
; Eight digits, low to high: the oldest written pronoun system in the set does not sort
; by sex; it is Sumerian, whose `ane` covers he and she alike; the oldest attested
; Indo-European (Hittite) does not sort pronouns by sex either; every pronoun here was
; written down before any human speech was ever recorded anywhere; every known
; feminine-pronoun coinage is modern-era; each lands at least four centuries into that
; culture's own literate history; sex-marked pronouns outside Indo-European and
; Afro-Asiatic number at most one in this sample; and Vedic's oral transmission outruns
; its own written attestation by more than a millennium.
;
; The asymmetry the question turns on: the written record differs per culture across
; 4200 years, and the spoken record is 1877 for every culture that has ever existed.
; Dates and forms are brought in by the rented voice, round and approximate by intent —
; no digit rests on a year being exact.
import "../pronoun-attestation-trace.fk"
